# Claude Code Configuration for MAC MCP Server
# This file provides context and rules for Claude and other AI agents working on this project

## Project Overview
project_name: Multi-Agent Coordination MCP Server
project_type: Python MCP Server
python_version: "3.12+"
architecture: Event-sourced orchestrator with Actor model

## Code Standards
- Use src/ layout for all production code
- All code must have type hints (mypy strict mode)
- Use async/await for all I/O operations
- Follow ruff linting rules (see pyproject.toml)
- Maintain test coverage above 90%
- Use property-based testing for core logic
- Document all public APIs with docstrings (Google style)

## Testing Requirements
- Unit tests: Fast, isolated, no I/O
- Integration tests: Test component interactions
- Property tests: Test invariants and edge cases
- All tests must be async-compatible
- Use pytest fixtures for setup/teardown
- Mock external dependencies in unit tests

## Architecture Principles
1. Event Sourcing: All state changes are events
2. Actor Model: Agents communicate via orchestrator only
3. State Machine: Explicit state transitions
4. Type Safety: Use Pydantic v2 for validation
5. Async First: All I/O is non-blocking

## File Organization
src/mac_mcp/
  ├── __init__.py          # Package exports and version
  ├── domain/              # Domain models (events, tasks, agents)
  ├── core/                # Core orchestrator logic
  ├── storage/             # Event store implementations
  ├── mcp/                 # MCP server and protocol
  └── cli.py               # CLI entry point

tests/
  ├── unit/                # Unit tests (fast, isolated)
  ├── integration/         # Integration tests
  └── property/            # Property-based tests

## Import Ordering
1. Standard library
2. Third-party packages
3. Local imports
(Enforced by ruff isort)

## Commit Standards
- Conventional commits format
- Run tests before committing
- No temporary/build files in commits
- Update documentation with code changes

## Documentation
- All docs have YAML frontmatter
- Keep README.md updated with API changes
- Update ARCHITECTURE.md for design changes
- Add examples for new features

## Security
- Never commit secrets or credentials
- Validate all external inputs
- Use type checking to prevent injection
- Rate limit external requests

## Performance
- Use uvloop on non-Windows systems
- Batch event writes when possible
- Implement backpressure in event streams
- Profile before optimizing

## AI Agent Guidance
When working on this codebase:
1. Read ARCHITECTURE.md and PROTOCOL.md first
2. Follow the patterns in existing code
3. Add tests for new functionality
4. Update type hints for all changes
5. Run `ruff check` and `mypy` before committing
6. Ensure all tests pass with `pytest`
7. Update relevant documentation
